15062.interface-spec.a1] defines state only after reset (RR-RESET). Every $past is guarded by a first-cycle flag. The assertions are immediate assertions in a clocked block, each guarded by an if, for the open Yosys front end. There are covers for every guard and every scenario.", "entry": "rr_arbiter_props"}], "tool_runs": [], "claims": ["The properties are expected to hold on the approved RTL. This expectation comes from reading the RTL by hand; no run has confirmed it.", "Hand analysis suggests the wait-counter and last!=3 helper invariants are enough for k-induction at depth 20. This is unconfirmed.", "The only assumption is reset in the first cycle. rst_n is unconstrained afterwards and req is unconstrained throughout."], "escalation": null, "notes": "The only assumption is that rst_n is low in cycle 0. Without it, last starts arbitrary and could be 3, a value the spec does not define. The spec says grant is combinational, so the grant properties are sampled on the clock edge, and every reachable post-reset state is checked there. RR-BOUND is read as follows: while req[i] stays high and rst_n stays high, requester i is not refused on more than two consecutive edges. A reset or a dropped req clears the counter. If formal.cover fails, check the cover for a full two-cycle wait (f_waitN == 2) first. Independent review is still required [evidence:prj-238bc15062.design-under-test.e1] [evidence:prj-238bc15062.interface-spec.e1]. Recommended follow-up: run these assertions against mutants of the RTL, for example a swapped priority arm or a missing hold."}

